  "content": {
    "blocks": [
      {
        "kind": "text",
        "body": {
          "en": "The advanced-math domain leans hard on quadratics: factoring, the quadratic formula, the vertex, and what the discriminant reveals.",
          "ar": null
        }
      },
      {
        "kind": "callout",
        "style": "thm",
        "label": {
          "en": "▲ Reference — Quadratics",
          "ar": null
        },
        "body": {
          "en": "\\( x=\\dfrac{-b\\pm\\sqrt{b^{2}-4ac}}{2a} \\); the discriminant \\( b^{2}-4ac \\) counts real solutions. The vertex of \\( y=ax^{2}+bx+c \\) is at \\( x=-\\dfrac{b}{2a} \\); factored form \\( a(x-r_1)(x-r_2) \\) shows the roots directly.",
          "ar": null
        }
      },
      {
        "kind": "cards",
        "items": [
          {
            "label": {
              "en": "Quadratic formula",
              "ar": null
            },
            "value": {
              "en": "\\( \\dfrac{-b\\pm\\sqrt{b^{2}-4ac}}{2a} \\)",
              "ar": null
            }
          },
          {
            "label": {
              "en": "Discriminant",
              "ar": null
            },
            "value": {
              "en": "\\( b^{2}-4ac \\)",
              "ar": null
            }
          },
          {
            "label": {
              "en": "Vertex",
              "ar": null
            },
            "value": {
              "en": "\\( x=-\\dfrac{b}{2a} \\)",
              "ar": null
            }
          },
          {
            "label": {
              "en": "Difference of squares",
              "ar": null
            },
            "value": {
              "en": "\\( a^{2}-b^{2} \\)",
              "ar": null
            }
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "kind": "figure",
        "figure": {
          "type": "fn",
          "title": "Quadratic Parabola: Vertex and X-Intercepts",
          "xrange": [-1, 5],
          "yrange": [-3, 6],
          "fns": [
            { "fn": "x^2 - 4*x + 3", "color": "var(--fig-indigo-2)", "label": "f(x) = x^2 - 4x + 3" }
          ],
          "points": [
            { "x": 1, "y": 0, "label": "Root (1, 0)" },
            { "x": 3, "y": 0, "label": "Root (3, 0)" },
            { "x": 2, "y": -1, "label": "Vertex (2, -1)" },
            { "x": 0, "y": 3, "label": "y-intercept (0, 3)" }
          ]
        },
        "caption": {
          "en": "Parabola $f(x) = x^2 - 4x + 3 = (x - 1)(x - 3) = (x - 2)^2 - 1$ showing the vertex $(2, -1)$, axis of symmetry $x = 2$, roots $(1, 0)$ and $(3, 0)$, and vertical intercept $(0, 3)$.",
          "ar": null
        }
      },
      {
        "kind": "callout",
        "style": "example",
        "label": {
          "en": "Worked example 3.A",
          "ar": null
        },
        "body": {
          "en": "<p>Solve \\( x^{2}-5x+6=0 \\).</p><p>\\( (x-2)(x-3)=0 \\):</p>\\[ x=2,\\ 3. \\]",
          "ar": null
        }
      },
      {
        "kind": "callout",
        "style": "example",
        "label": {
          "en": "Worked example 3.B",
          "ar": null
        },
        "body": {
          "en": "<p>State the vertex of \\( y=(x-3)^{2}+2 \\).</p>\\[ (3,\\,2). \\]",
          "ar": null
        }
      },
      {
        "kind": "callout",
        "style": "def",
        "label": {
          "en": "▲ Absolute Value Equations &amp; Inequalities",
          "ar": null
        },
        "body": {
          "en": "Solve \\(|ax+b|=c\\) by splitting into <em>two</em> linear equations: \\(ax+b=c\\) and \\(ax+b=-c\\). Always check both. For inequalities: \\(|x|&lt;k\\;\\Rightarrow\\;-k&lt;x&lt;k\\) (and-compound); \\(|x|&gt;k\\;\\Rightarrow\\;x&lt;-k\\;\\text{or}\\;x&gt;k\\) (or-compound). No solution if right side is negative.",
          "ar": null
        }
      },
      {
        "kind": "cards",
        "items": [
          {
            "label": {
              "en": "\\(|f(x)|=k\\)",
              "ar": null
            },
            "value": {
              "en": "Two cases: \\(f(x)=\\pm k\\)",
              "ar": null
            }
          },
          {
            "label": {
              "en": "\\(|x|&lt;k\\)",
              "ar": null
            },
            "value": {
              "en": "\\(-k&lt;x&lt;k\\)",
              "ar": null
            }
          },
          {
            "label": {
              "en": "\\(|x|&gt;k\\)",
              "ar": null
            },
            "value": {
              "en": "\\(x&lt;{-k}\\) or \\(x&gt;k\\)",
              "ar": null
            }
          },
          {
            "label": {
              "en": "No solution",
              "ar": null
            },
            "value": {
              "en": "\\(|f(x)|=\\text{negative}\\)",
              "ar": null
            }
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "kind": "callout",
        "style": "example",
        "label": {
          "en": "Worked example 3.C — Absolute value equation",
          "ar": null
        },
        "body": {
          "en": "<p>Solve \\(|2x-3|=7\\).</p><p><strong>Case 1:</strong> \\(2x-3=7\\Rightarrow x=5\\).</p><p><strong>Case 2:</strong> \\(2x-3=-7\\Rightarrow x=-2\\).</p><p>Both check out. Solutions: \\(x=5\\) or \\(x=-2\\).</p>",
          "ar": null
        }
      },
      {
        "kind": "callout",
        "style": "def",
        "label": {
          "en": "▲ Nonlinear Systems &amp; Graphs",
          "ar": null
        },
        "body": {
          "en": "A linear–quadratic system can have 0, 1, or 2 solutions — determined by the discriminant after substitution. Substitute the linear expression for \\(y\\) into the quadratic, rearrange to standard form, and solve or test the discriminant. For a line and circle, the same method applies.",
          "ar": null
        }
      },
      {
        "kind": "callout",
        "style": "example",
        "label": {
          "en": "Worked example 3.D — Line–parabola system",
          "ar": null
        },
        "body": {
          "en": "<p>Find all intersections of \\(y=x^{2}\\) and \\(y=x+2\\).</p><p>Set equal: \\(x^{2}=x+2\\Rightarrow x^{2}-x-2=0\\Rightarrow(x-2)(x+1)=0\\).</p>\\[x=2,\\;y=4\\qquad\\text{and}\\qquad x=-1,\\;y=1.\\]",
          "ar": null
        }
      },
      {
        "kind": "explorer",
        "title": {
          "en": "⊙ Explorer · Line–parabola intersections",
          "ar": null
        },
        "canvasId": "satQuadLineCanvas",
        "ariaLabel": {
          "en": "The parabola y equals x squared and a line, with their intersection points marked",
          "ar": null
        },
        "dataPanel": {
          "btnId": "satQuadLineDataBtn",
          "panelId": "satQuadLineDataPanel",
          "descId": "satQuadLineDataDesc",
          "rowsId": "satQuadLineDataRows",
          "caption": {
            "en": "The intersection point(s) of y = x² and the line",
            "ar": null
          },
          "columns": [
            { "en": "x", "ar": null },
            { "en": "y", "ar": null }
          ]
        },
        "controls": [
          {
            "id": "satQuadLineM",
            "label": { "en": "slope <b>m</b>:", "ar": null },
            "min": -6, "max": 6, "value": 1, "step": 0.5,
            "valueDisplayId": "satQuadLineMval",
            "ariaLabel": { "en": "Line slope m", "ar": null }
          },
          {
            "id": "satQuadLineK",
            "label": { "en": "intercept <b>k</b>:", "ar": null },
            "min": -8, "max": 8, "value": 2, "step": 0.5,
            "valueDisplayId": "satQuadLineKval",
            "ariaLabel": { "en": "Line y-intercept k", "ar": null }
          }
        ],
        "readouts": [
          {
            "id": "satQuadLineD",
            "label": { "en": "discriminant (after substitution)", "ar": null },
            "colorClass": "amber",
            "value": "9.00"
          },
          {
            "id": "satQuadLineN",
            "label": { "en": "number of solutions", "ar": null },
            "colorClass": "indigo",
            "value": "2"
          }
        ],
        "note": {
          "en": "y = x² is fixed (indigo); the line y = mx + k is amber. Default m = 1, k = 2 is exactly Worked example 3.D (intersections at (2, 4) and (−1, 1)). Substituting gives x² − mx − k = 0 — drag the sliders until its discriminant crosses zero and watch the two intersection points merge into one, then disappear.",
          "ar": null
        }
      },
      {
        "kind": "callout",
        "style": "warn",
        "label": {
          "en": "⚠️ SAT Traps & Pitfalls — Quadratics & Vertex Form",
          "ar": null
        },
        "body": {
          "en": "<ul><li><strong>Minimum/Maximum Value Trap:</strong> When asked for the <em>minimum or maximum value</em> of a quadratic, the answer is the $y$-coordinate of the vertex ($k$), NOT the $x$-value ($h$) where it occurs.</li><li><strong>Vieta's Formula Shortcuts:</strong> For $ax^2 + bx + c = 0$, the sum of roots is $-b/a$ and the product of roots is $c/a$. Memorizing these saves minutes over factoring or using the quadratic formula!</li><li><strong>Discriminant Zero / Tangency:</strong> 'Exactly one real solution' or 'intersects at exactly one point' means $b^2 - 4ac = 0$. 'No real solutions' means $b^2 - 4ac &lt; 0$.</li><li><strong>Vertex Form Signs:</strong> In $y = a(x - h)^2 + k$, the vertex is $(h, k)$. For $y = 3(x + 5)^2 - 4$, the vertex is $(-5, -4)$, NOT $(+5, -4)$.</li></ul>",
          "ar": null
        }
      },
      {
        "kind": "callout",
        "style": "example",
        "label": {
          "en": "Exam Practice 3.E — Vieta's Formula & Vertex Value",
          "ar": null
        },
        "body": {
          "en": "<p><strong>Question:</strong> What is the sum of the solutions to $3x^2 - 18x + 7 = 0$, and what is the minimum value of $f(x) = 3x^2 - 18x + 7$?</p><p><strong>Solution:</strong></p><ol><li><strong>Sum of solutions:</strong> By Vieta's formulas, $\\text{Sum} = -\\frac{b}{a} = -\\frac{-18}{3} = 6$.</li><li><strong>Minimum value:</strong> The vertex $x$-coordinate is $h = -\\frac{b}{2a} = -\\frac{-18}{2(3)} = 3$. The minimum value is $f(3) = 3(3)^2 - 18(3) + 7 = 27 - 54 + 7 = -20$.</li></ol>",
          "ar": null
        }
      },
      {
        "kind": "practiceProblem",
        "question": {
          "en": "<strong>SAT-style:</strong> How many solutions does the system \\(y=x^{2}-4x+4\\) and \\(y=2x-3\\) have?",
          "ar": null
        },
        "solution": {
          "en": "Substitute: \\(x^2-4x+4=2x-3\\Rightarrow x^2-6x+7=0\\). Discriminant \\(=36-28=8&gt;0\\), so <strong>two solutions</strong>.",
          "ar": null
        }
      }
    ]
  },
